the power of the cuteness until it turns you into a smiling zombie. However, some of the meeps are actually dangerous and have things like razor fangs and magical attacks... also they are edible and quite tasty, which is why the mutated non dangerous variety are something of an endangered species.</div> </div> </div> </div>That sounds exactly like something I would expect to find in the Hedge. Or as a Cryptid. Or some fluffy animal that has been Claimed by a spirit of cuteness/love. Basically, I want this in every game line now.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-21T11:40:11-07:00","id":850714,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=850701#post850701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That sounds exactly like something I would expect to find in the Hedge. Or as a Cryptid. Or some fluffy animal that has been Claimed by a spirit of cuteness/love. Basically, I want this in every game line now.</div> </div> </div> </div>They originally showed up in GURPS IOU, which is a cross-over comedy setting that takes place at a university for students with paranormal abilities, mythical creatures, study abroad students from other, planets, times and universes (the history school at the university uses time travel and alternate universe visits as field trips and the history school's dean is a parody of the 4th doctor.) and many other weird characters. In it, the Meeps were a species that infested the campus a few years back and nobody knew where they came from and were named for their resemblance to a &quot;moopsball&quot; which was used in the main sport at the university.<br /> <br /> It is a very silly setting to say the least.<br /> <br /> The meeps also tend to come in swarms of dozens to hundreds.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T07:41:45-07:00","id":852426,"title":"","content":"Currently don't have that book, but probably good for the next cycle of potential write-ups.<br /> <br /> Doing Cows from Space.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T11:54:06-07:00","id":852519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leliel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=852426#post852426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Currently don't have that book, but probably good for the next cycle of potential write-ups.<br /> <br /> Doing Cows from Space.</div> </div> </div> </div>That also sounds amazing <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Cursory research on meeps indicates that they don't originate in the IOU; they show up first in <i>The War Against the Cthorr</i> sourcebook. (Also they may have been precursors to Tribbles.) Leliel, could I do a write-up/translation for them and put it here? If you want to do them yourself/keep this thread dedicated to your conversions/keep this thread dedicated to monsters that originated in GURPS/say no for whatever other reason, I won't.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T12:02:30-07:00","id":852528,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=852519#post852519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That also sounds amazing <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Cursory research on meeps indicates that they don't originate in the IOU; they show up first in <i>The War Against the Cthorr</i> sourcebook. (Also they may have been precursors to Tribbles.) Leliel, could I do a write-up/translation for them and put it here? If you want to do them yourself/keep this thread dedicated to your conversions/keep this thread dedicated to monsters that originated in GURPS/say no for whatever other reason, I won't.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're right, but the IOU meeps are shaped different (like a half cantaloupe with feet) and the Cthorr meeps don't come in the dangerous mutant varieties, so while they're clearly inspired by the Cthorr meeps, I think they are sufficiently different to be their own thing.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-24T15:08:00-07:00","id":852628,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=852519#post852519\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That also sounds amazing <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Cursory research on meeps indicates that they don't originate in the IOU; they show up first in <i>The War Against the Cthorr</i> sourcebook. (Also they may have been precursors to Tribbles.) Leliel, could I do a write-up/translation for them and put it here? If you want to do them yourself/keep this thread dedicated to your conversions/keep this thread dedicated to monsters that originated in GURPS/say no for whatever other reason, I won't.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Do as you will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-29T18:39:54-07:00","id":855413,"title":"","content":"Special April Fool's Day Entry:<br /> <br /> <b>Cows From Space</b><br /> <br /> <i>&lt;&lt;Pathetic human farmer. In time your meat will serve the People as protein, and you shall labor to till our fields. The cud of the world is in our mouth, and the udders of wrath shall drown you in the tears of our mothers and our righteous vengeance! &gt;&gt;</i><br /> <br /> Soviet Russia was not a country known for scientific ethics. Enough of a lack of ethics they stopped actually making many scientific advancements (to use a historically dubious but characteristic example, telling the guy who wants to create a race of human/chimpanzee hybrids off is just as much a matter of common sense as it is morality). But ethics do get in the way of good (in the sense of actual potential for scientific gain, not in the sense of not causing queasiness among people with consciences) ideas, sometimes. In October 1958, a strange reading appeared in the sky above the Ural Mountains, not far from where, for no apparent reason, nine otherwise experiences hikers vanished, leaving only heavily damaged equipment behind. Not trusting this to be a coincidence, the Soviets immediately assigned a clandestine project to watch the phenomenon, including at least one psychic of the notorious Iron Comrade project. Upon said arrival, said psychic, an unassuming Lithuanian rancher by trade named Emilis Januitis immediately reported feeling emanations from the softly glowing &quot;comet&quot;. describing it as &quot;the vellum scent of profound age, but also the heartbeat of an unborn fetus&quot; (Emilis really wanted to be a poet). Long story short, when the anomaly remained there, the Soviets realized their meticulous observations were going nowhere, and so, with characteristic pragmatism, they decided to poke it with a very high-tech (at the time) stick and see what happened. <br /> <br /> Of course, if you don't have something on your stick that can be affected by the thing being poked, you can't tell exactly what said poke-ee is doing. So, to test for effects on living organic matter, the Soviets put one of Emilis' cattle on the rocket they were using, along with several varieties of inorganic matter-lead, iron, sand, gold, and an oxygen tank. The experiment proved to be a success, but not in any way the Russians wanted- as soon as the rocket touched the comet-like anomaly, both vanished utterly-along with all of the pictures of the comet. The team lurked around Ural for a few months after that, but eventually other dead-end projects called, and the team dispersed.<br /> <br /> The cow regards this as one of the first times she saw how despicable a species humanity was. What she can recall, anyway-her brain was not meant for remembering faces at that point. Or words. Or really anything apart from where the good grass was, but she's pretty sure she saw Emilis laughing in her face...she thinks.<br /> <br /> The anomaly, as it turns out, was a passing angel of the God-Machine, there to examine the strange energies unleashed in the Ural incident, and was so lost in its work that is didn't notice the rocket coming at it until it hit the angel in the olfactory detector. Surprised, the angel instinctively teleported back to its lunar Infrastructure, taking the rocket with it. While the angel was recalled on the basis of discovery, the Machine's sensors didn't detect the cow, which was thrown into the storage Infrastructure and the temporal stasis field meant entirely for inorganic matter-rather than sending her into an unmoving stasis, it vastly accelerated her personal timeline, 30 years in an hour-but she was lucky. Her biology proved compatible with Aether, and within her body, new biology formed-an infinitely better set of organelles for repairing the damage caused by age among them. Her brain grew exponentially more complex, developing an enlarged frontal lobe. Nerves throughout her body grew into tangles of unique pseudo-organs capable of manipulating space. Her growing intellect grew fat on the streams of information within the Machine's heart-and she grew wroth with rage. Over the course of, from the Earth's perspective, next week, she studied the God-Machine's intelligence on the species she blamed for sealing her in space for thousands of years, eventually mastering a hack by which she tricked the Machine's Infrastructure into cloning her, with enough genetic modification to be considered unrelated members of the same species. To these fetuses of the newly dubbed <i>Bos supremus, </i>she taught her knowledge of the world that slaughtered their dumb cousins for meat, and stole the milk meant for their calves. Finally, she used her growing knowledge of spatial relations to open portals all over the world-and all over the world. ranchers shrugged at the sudden addition of new heifers and quietly added them to their expected profit margins, unknowing of the cold and alien intellects behind their unexpected fortune.<br /> <br /> The Age of the Cow From Space has arrived, and soon, all the world shall bow before their bovoid supremacy! <br /> <br /> Any day now. <br /> <br /> Despite them not putting any plan into action<br /> <br /> For the past half-a-century...<br /> <br /> Okay, let's face it; hyper-intelligent, psychic cattle with omnivorous diets are still, well, <i>domestic cattle. </i>They aren't exactly the most aggressive species on the face of the planet, with even undocked bulls only charging when you provoke them. Even the Supreme Oxen Empress herself is mostly motivated over anger as what she perceives as a personal attack-now that Emilis is dead (from falling out of a hole in the sky), she's lost a great deal of her rage. For the most part, Cows From Space prefer the quiet life, chewing cud and dreaming of the inevitable victory when they finally put their plans into motion. But provocation is something that creeps up on humans very easily, and they are psychic, omniverous, and hyper-intelligent. How many meat packing plant employees have died for seemingly completely normal reasons recently?<br /> <br /> \u200b(will continue, have other obligations)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-15T06:28:03-07:00","id":891116,"title":"","content":"(Finally, finally continuing)<br /> <br /> <b>Cow From Space:</b><br /> <br /> <u>Attributes:</u><br /> <br /> <b>Mental: </b>Intelligence 8, Wits 2, Resolve 7<br /> <br /> <b>Physical: </b>Strength 4, Dexterity 3, Stamina 5<br /> <br /> <b>Social: </b>Presence 3, Manipulation 4, Composure 1<br /> <br /> <u>Skills:</u><br /> <br /> <b>Mental:</b> Academics 3 (Agriculture), Crafts 2 (Slave Oversight), Investigation 4, Occult 1, Science 5 (Local-Scale Doomsday Devices)<br /> <br /> <b>Physical: </b>Athletics 3, Brawl 2 (Have Skull, Will Use), Stealth 2 (Perfectly Normal Cow), Survival 2<br /> <br /> <b>Social: </b>Animal Ken 1 (Barnyard Animals), Intimidation 3, Subterfuge 2<br /> <br /> <u>Merits</u>: Common Sense ***, Eidetic Memory **, Patient *, Thief of Fate ***<br /> <br /> <u>Potency:</u> 4<br /> <br /> <u>Willpower</u>: 8<br /> <br /> <u>Virtue</u>: Ambitious<br /> <br /> <u>Vice</u>: Lazy<br /> <br /> <u>Aspirations</u>: Take over the world (someday), avenge cruel treatment, have a nice, sedate life.<br /> <br /> <u>Speed: </u>12<br /> <br /> <u>Health:</u> 10<br /> <br /> <u>Initiative:</u> 4<br /> <br /> <u>Defense</u>: 5<br /> <br /> <u>Bans</u>: Cows from Space are blowhards, and are psychologically incapable of not boasting to an apparently helpless or seemingly allied human. Roll a d10 and divide by 2, rounding up-the number is the amount of pertinent facts the Cow unknowingly reveals. In addition, they will not actually attempt to kill someone except as part of their &quot;avenge cruel treatment&quot; Aspiration.<br /> <br /> <u>Dread Powers:</u><br /> <br /> Emotional Aura (Numen), Influence (Spatial Anomalies *****), Know Soul. While Cows from Space are telepathic and capable of understanding all languages of people they've used Know Soul on, it their aggressive telepathic abilities are covered by Emotional Aura-they can't actively dominate someone through sheer psychic force.<br /> <br /> (Will Continue, posting this so I can update Chrome without losing work).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 05-15-2016, 03:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-15T17:00:53-07:00","id":891448,"title":"","content":"<b>Storytelling Hints: </b>First-give up on having an entirely serious mood if you are using <i>Bos supremus </i>as figures in a story. Any attempt to make intelligent cattle seem terrifying is going to be undercut by the fact they are, indeed, cattle. If your troupe happens to have anything resembling a sense of humor, all trying to sell the Cows from Space as legitimately horrific is going to make the actual concept seem more and more like a farce. That's all fine and dandy if that's the purpose, but really, you don't invoke cattle in dramatic moments and expect people to not acknowledge they are, well, cattle.<br /> <br /> Still, this does tie into the overall theme of a story featuring Cows from Space, that of hiding in plain sight. The very fact that the idea of cryptid, megalomaniacal barnyard animals is so utterly ridiculous nobody takes it seriously out of universe means that nobody in the CofD takes it seriously either-even before the evolved bovines start bombarding the players' potential allies with complacent feelings, proving that Bessie is in fact an evil genius with a talent for weapons design is an uphill battle, to say the least. Add in the fact that <i>Bos supremus </i>is a species born capable of playing with time-space in ways that even Mastigos wizards at the peak of their powers find jaw-dropping, it's quite likely that the instant the Cows get wind of their imminent exposure, they'll teleport to the other side of the world and leave normal bovines behind, leaving their would-be revealers to look insane, stupid, or most likely, both. Assuming they don't use said spatial manipulations to interfere without ever tipping their hooves-it's (not particularly) surprising what you can do with gravity and the ability to make heavy objects suddenly several feet high, directly over an investigator's car full of evidence. On the other side of the story equation, the Cows are the very embodiment of stupid smart people-they have a lot of raw brainpower, but they're <i>horrible </i>at guessing at what a human is going to do next. Know Soul is no substitute for emotional intelligence, and thus they are repeatedly flabbergasted by humans behaving in ways not directly driven by Aspiration, Virtue, or Vice-daily life, in other words.<br /> <br /> Still, there is a mood that lends itself to a more somber undertone when dealing with <i>Bos supremus</i>; seemingly harmless things having terrible implications. On the one hand, this applies to the evolved bovoids themselves, supervillains that look like completely normal meat stock, but it also applies to their origin story; who in their right minds would say putting a living cow on a rocket, like many other early experiments into reaching space, would result in such creatures? It also figures into what snaps them out of their instinctive complacency-as implied by the more pacifistic side of their Ban, Cows from Space are generally shaken out of their complacent instincts by someone behaving viciously. When it comes to farms and cattle, that generally means the excesses of the meat and dairy industry. While Cows from Space accept the fact that their dumb cousins are meant for protein, and sadly accept that (they don't eat beef for the most part, but it's more for the same squeamishnes humans don't eat chimp meat than any spiritual sense of kinship-they don't really object), they despise animal cruelty, especially the kind that incidentally targets the evolved bovoids. Thing is though, most animal cruelty caused by industrial farms is caused by obliviousness rather than malice; products of money cutbacks, inattentiveness, and just plain laziness. This does not excuse behaviors like leaving animals to stew in their own filth for months simply because the staff cleaners didn't feel like it, but it does emphasize the fact that a simple act coming more from lack of effort results in a great deal of misery for a bunch of innocent livestock-and not-so-innocent farmers, if the Cows from Space happen to be grazing there at the time. Who knows how many disasters that struck meatpacking towns are as much the product of revenge as ill luck?<br /> <br /> (Don't have the mental effort for different gamelines involving them. Be my guest)/<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-25T13:22:42-07:00","id":899325,"title":"","content":"New day, new cycle of votes. This theme is &quot;body horror&quot;:<br /> <br /> <b>Darklings: </b>Not to be confused with the Lost Seeming of the same name, and in fact they're probably the opposite of <i>those </i>Darklings. Rather than being a rebel against alien slavers whose guilt over her actions has caused her to absorb something of the fretful, frightening dark, Darklings are the still human servants of an alien dark-and most of the time, they don't know. The &quot;darksome&quot;, it seems, recognizes a cult of organ thieves who willingly signed up and are fully aware of it are probably not subtle enough for its purposes. So instead, it finds people of particular social groupings (which depends on brood to brood-the darksome isn't choosy) with a hidden darkness in their past. Through that darkness, the darksome reaches and stains the souls of the humans that are members of it, creating a nocturnal split personality in all of them with three goals: Serve the darksome, serve their Vices, and steal internal organs to implant in gardens to farm (yes, they replace the organs with shadowy prosthetics, but <i>still</i>). This isn't due to the darksome's grotesque sense of aesthetics; Like any crop, the viscera gardens are meant for feeding, and in time, the power sent to the darklings' shadowy master will allow it to swallow them, and anyone nearby.<br /> <br /> <b>Fabricants: </b><i>Battlestar Galactica</i> was right-there are machines clothed in human flesh about, and they hate us. Of course, they were wrong about their origins-fabricants weren't created by men, they <i>are </i>men-or were. Perhaps it's a side-effect of existing outside the normal flow of the fourth dimension, perhaps it's the Abyss, perhaps AM exists in the future and wants at least <i>somebody </i>who understands him, but occasionally, someone organic will become &quot;unstuck&quot; in time, usually due to a disastrous attempt at time travel. Over the course of a few months their internal organs turn to circuitry, silicon, and metal, and their minds turn towards disgust with anything built from hydrocarbons (to the point where they are always looking for opportunities to take off their disgusting &quot;skin&quot; disguise and let it all hang out). Some just turn to hermitage and tech support over the phone, others decide Skynet isn't being invented fast enough and start methodical extermination of anything that harms a poor, innocent machine. Most dangerous of all are those who retain some empathy, still care about some people and animals-they're the ones who &quot;liberate&quot; their organic companions into fabricant existence, and from there form cabals of vicious, technically gifted cyborgs.<br /> <br /> <b>The Flayed: </b>People tend to underappreciate skin. It's an understandable error-it's there from the day you were born, and unless there's a whole in it, you get used to it. The Flayed appreciate skin all too well-they're constantly in danger of losing all of it. Victims of a supernaturally mutated form of leprosy, the Flayed lose a few square inches every hour, on the hour, it literally peeling off and falling off almost painlessly. Almost, because eventually, some skin that's keeping things like &quot;muscle,&quot; &quot;guts,&quot;, &quot;your heart&quot; in is going to fall off, and the Flayed dies a horrible death. Thankfully for them, there's a way to treat it so the disease does not progress for a few blessed hours, a day, or even grow some skin back. Unfortunately for the planet, that's &quot;drive people insane with fear&quot;-the only thing that sates the Flayed disease, it seems, is trauma, and given how Flayed become utterly fearless in a short while, that means anyone nearby should probably pay for PTSD treatment in advance. The disease is merciful in one way, though-it gives the Flayed exactly the powers they need to do that...<br /> <br /> <br /> Special vote: I also own GURPS Fantasy II: Madlands. If people want to, I can convert a monster or two (whether they be Footless, Soulless, or Heightless) from there at the end of this cycle.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-25T14:24:16-07:00","id":899397,"title":"","content":"I vote darklings. I want to see what an organ-garden looks like, as well as having a new 'mysterious, evil benefactor' character.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/forum/core/<img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=423\" data-tempid=\"temp_423_1523360155059_212\" />\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10397-ajf115","name":"ajf115","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10397_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2416"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-25T14:44:40-07:00","id":899418,"title":"","content":"Darkling or Fabricant. I don't know anything about Madlands, but more stuff is always cool!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-25T16:05:17-07:00","id":899502,"title":"","content":"I think I'll cast my vote on Fabricants.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-26T02:54:56-07:00","id":899887,"title":"","content":"I was hoping you'd do fabricants, so my vote is for the infectious nudist robots. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-27T07:12:32-07:00","id":900923,"title":"","content":"Fabricants is my vote!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-27T08:38:41-07:00","id":901010,"title":"","content":"I vote <b>Fabricants</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-27T14:53:14-07:00","id":901428,"title":"","content":"Voting's closed, fabricants win by a yard.<br /> <br /> Will be posting soon enough.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-04T17:58:44-07:00","id":907349,"title":"","content":"PROTIP: When you schedule a project to be written on the side, make sure you have the kind of attention span that doesn't focus on one thing at a time!<br /> <br /> Finally:<br /> <br /> <b>Fabricants </b>(aka Futurians, Technogores)<br /> <br /> <i>Relax. Chew. It will be much easier for your liberation than it was for me. Once you are made pure of hydrocarbon-oh, the things we shall do. Together. Like before.</i><br /> <br /> If the past is a foreign country, then the future is another planet. Not only are no humans existent there, no human has ever <i>been </i>there, by definition. It hasn't happened yet, and to be frank, the only way we present folk can even imagine it is to speculate. Usually in ways that often does not remotely resemble the future, even from people who specialize in speculating what the future looks like. How many times has it been said humanity's technological development has reached its end state? In the past fifty years? From the other side, how many movies from around the 1980s have the 2000s as a technological wasteland of deserts? Truly, the future is an alien world, and anything of it is even more alien.<br /> <br /> Is that why fabricants hate the people of the present so much? Are their mechanical parts born of a potential future, one they know will never arrive in even their extendable lifetimes? Is the loathing that pulses through their oil-pump hearts really born not of any inherent nature, but of loneliness and anger for the world they can no longer be part of, and the world that they could call home never reached? It certainly explains their more infectious behavior, and also why they don't seem intent so much on the extermination of organic life as avoiding it.<br /> <br /> But the writer digresses. Because the part that others recognize in fabricants, what makes them so mutually horrifying, is that the hateful androids aren't from the carbon-less future, not really. Once upon a time, they were humans, friends and family, husbands and wives. All that is gone now, subsumed into silicon and steel-and all their soul consumed by hatred for not only the species they once were, but anything that reminds them of fleshy existence. Even their own skins.<br /> <br /> Fabricants are, in shorthand, organic beings who have become almost entirely mechanical forms of the people and animals they once were, with the only parts that aren't whirring machinery being their now leathery skins, which can be shed and replaced as easily as a human takes his favorite suit on and off. The machine only resembles the being it used to be in exterior frame-because the machine is also scientifically possible by largely mundane principles of engineering. Sure, some of the computational units in the brain-case have occult symbols etched in their circuitry that allow quantum computing, and the power source is never quite clear where it's getting the energy from to convert what they eat into battery power, but everything else? Completely in the bounds of hypothetical near-future robotics and engineering (no matter where they show up-a less advanced are may find itself plagued with fabricants that have LED eyes and piston muscles, while the First World's native technogores have sleek insides that have as much to do with nanotech as larger-scale metalwork).<br /> <br /> All resemble their previous selves, except in one way-utter, gut-level contempt for any form of mobile carbon-based life. Even their skins come off as being too much like the bipedal <i>things </i>they have to switch off their olfactory sensors to deal with every damn day. In fact, one of the weaknesses of a futurian infestation is that they take the opportunity to remove their skins the moment they feel safely private from any prying eyes, allowing their circuits to cool in the air. This distaste colors literally <i>everything </i>they do and think; the transformation into a fabricant breaks all casual connection with anything organic under the sheer force of disgust; fabricants who were loners in their previous life happily become highly-advanced hermits, the only company needed or wanted being whatever machines they can scrounge up to serve as decoration and spare parts. The ones who were <i>closely </i>connected still love their families-but that love is twisted, constantly battling with the nauseous feeling in the stomach-furnaces that the fabricants suffer whenever they think about anything except the most cerebral aspects of their loved ones' existence.<br /> <br /> Those same loved ones are usually patients one through ten when the fabricants discover they can &quot;liberate&quot; others into the same mechanical existence.<br /> <br /> How a particular &quot;code&quot; of fabricants (the accepted term for a particular strain of the infection) arises is perhaps the most mysterious aspect about them, not the least because there actually is one exact thing in common: the proto-fabricant becomes &quot;unstuck&quot; in time for at least three seconds-not just frozen in time or alternatively moving when time is stopped around her, but actually experiences the passing of time while <i>outside </i>conventional time (a disorienting experience, to say the least). Unsurprisingly, this is incredibly rare, usually involving the very definition of a freak series of events or deliberate engineering (and before you ask, no, fabricants themselves almost never have enough resources or expertise in temporal phenomenon to engineer said events). When the proto-fabricant comes back, they seem completely fine-at first. Over the course of a few months, the mind of the proto-fabricant begins to dwell on both the fragility of organic life, and its flaws. The fundamental uselessness of breathing in everyday life, for example, or how instinct that overrides compassion and reason are the results of chemicals not released by the brain. Many proto-fabricants shake off these thoughts, and the transformation ends there. Some, whether weak-willed or already misanthropic, come to agree with this viewpoint, which naturally leads into the devaluation of the benefits of organic life (&quot;Who was the bright spark that thought touch-and thus pain-was such a good thing?&quot;), a growing annoyance with uncontrolled emotion or inconveniences produced by frailty, and eventually, the revelation that organic life is not only flawed, but inherently disgusting-not worth the complexity of the molecules it's born from. With every step, the proto-fabricant's insides reconfigure and calcify a little more, wires replacing nerves, newly steel joints growing tiny motors, the heart being recycled for the nanotubes of a fuel-efficient carburetor. By the time of the final revelation, the brain itself turns to crystalline superconductors of an unknown silicon-platinum composite, and a new technogore stalks and clanks through the night.<br /> <br /> Even people who only know the new futurian can tell the gradual change in the fabricant's personality. While the transformation does not rob her of any emotional capacity (logic actually <i>requires </i>emotion-otherwise the mind lacks the ability to assign good feelings, and thus value, to any given option, and thus the mind cannot make decisions at all, let alone logical ones), the growing disgust does manifest in increasing coldness and distance towards anything and everything not a fabricant or a less-advanced machine. It's simply not prudent to reveal the fabricant's actual feelings, and the bile is so great that any intelligent fabricant (which is all of them) recognizes that it may creep into anything but the most aloof behavior. They often take to wearing sunglasses to hide any static or damage to their video-screen eyes, and rarely open their mouths entirely, as the teeth have become ceramic and the tongue wiggling rubber studded with sensors for temperature and mineral composition. They become more technically skilled, the gradual transformation of the brain from grey matter to CPU mainframe coming with programs that direct the fabricant in the esoteric engineering needed to repair themselves, which naturally includes a great deal of knowledge on programming anything less advanced than their own makeup-they need to understand much of &quot;relic&quot; technology in case they ever need to incorporate it into themselves as a replacement. Most, of course, do not notice-and there is no &quot;but&quot; addendum for loved ones, not really. Fabricants aren't made sociopaths, and thus when faced with an organic they actually care for, they are able to swallow their disgust by focusing entirely on non-physical aspect of their relationship-they may come off as depressed, and they recoil at touch or the thought of sexuality, but the latter isn't that frequently brought up, and the stigma around mental illness means many ignore the former.<br /> <br /> Under the skin and the forced smile, though, the fabricants are lonely creatures. Surrounded by creatures that revolt them simply by the beating of their hearts, feeling only comforted by the hum of machines and tolerating plants alone among all living things (most fabricants run on biofuel), technogores are repelled by social contact. For some, that's fine-many fabricants were misanthropic loners before their transformation, and so all they need to do to maintain their spare part budget is find a stay-at-home job of their choice, preferably in the most deserted area they can find-easy enough, for a genus of natural engineers and programmers. The more social ones, however, are torn-they <i>remember </i>the days that they could look at a family member and not feel as though they were stepping in dog shit. Thus, they swallow their distaste, and resign to an existence of misery...<br /> <br /> Then, one day, they get the impulse to feed some of their skin to an animal. Maybe a rat, an insect, the family dog. They don't know why, they just cut a bit of the grimly necessary disguise off, watch it regenerate, and then force feed it. And then they watch, rapt, as the animal's skin becomes loose-fitting, then falls off to reveal another advanced robot!<br /> <br /> Whatever eldritch mechanism that causes &quot;liberation&quot;, as fabricants term it, it still seems to exist in their skin disguises. Actually, it exists everywhere in the fabricant, but the skin is the most easily healed and palatable. Ingesting it starts the metamorphosis into a fabricant of the same technological background as the vector, but also links their minds to the fabricant's disgust for life, bombarding them with every vicious invective and feeling the futurian has realized about organic matter. The two sync well, and what took the original fabricant a matter of months is shortened to <i>hours </i>among those who partake of fabricant flesh. Yes, it's possible to resist the tide of anger, to fight off the infection, but technogores don't liberate everyone-only people they view as useful allies, or more importantly people they would love, if it wasn't for their fleshy bits. Generally, a fabricant knows just what to think to get her infected to agree with her premises on the fallibility of carbon vs the perfection of metal, and if the soft method doesn't work, well there's always brainwashing. Upon the full transformation, a child-technogore feels instinctive loyalty to his code of fabricants, &quot;us against the world.&quot; From there, fabricants quickly convert those closest to them-and almost invariably, start looking into spreading their perfect condition among others, cults of a dark transhumanism that would happily destroy most of the world to rid it of awful, disgusting flesh (though fabricants do become more logical, and realize it's probably best to leave at least some humans organic-besides the fact that some people <i>deserve </i>to be weak, they don't know how to build babies). They're usually caught before they cause any global damage, they're not as subtle as they'd like to be-but what they <i>do </i>leave is bad enough, including mechanical beasts that share the same hatred for organic life.<br /> <br /> As a final insult to injury, though, it <i>is </i>possible to cure a fabricant, and turn them back into flesh that has no particular grudge against life. It's just that, besides being just as arcane as the strains of infection arise, and likely unique to each code, the fabricant has to be convinced to let go of hate, and accept guilt for her actions. Ask anyone who has dealt with prejudice about how easy that is.<br /> <br /> (will continue later)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-05T04:46:56-07:00","id":907565,"title":"","content":"Are you having the replicants be a condition?<br /> <br /> <br /> I mean, the sample character in the book was a vampire replicant, and well, in cofd, I imagine by and far it's supernaturals who are more likely to get unstuck in time...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-05T08:46:07-07:00","id":907657,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=907565#post907565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Are you having the replicants be a condition?<br /> <br /> <br /> I mean, the sample character in the book was a vampire replicant, and well, in cofd, I imagine by and far it's supernaturals who are more likely to get unstuck in time...</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yes and no.<br /> <br /> Pure fabricants, those who were born as mortals, are probably Horrors in their own right, and it's supernaturals who are also likely resistant to the infection. So it's a condition, but supernatural fabricants are actually significantly weaker.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-06T05:31:51-07:00","id":908274,"title":"","content":"Fair enough, I'd have just done it that there's a limit to how much energy and time a supernatural replicant has, so say a mage replicant that devotes itself into becoming the terminator just has that less time and effort to master their arcana, as an example. Even though npcs don't really keep track of experience, I know. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I was thinking about what might happen to a supernatural once they become a replicant.....<br /> <br /> Vampire- A psychotic disgust with all things organic doesn't really lead to keeping a humanity score, I imagine that vampiric replicants quickly become druagr. They might form a new bloodline though.<br /> <br /> Werewolf- I think a werewolf replicant will infect the rest of its pack if not killed first, and the new replicant pack would look for a new totem if their old one was too flesh-like.<br /> <br /> Mage- I think a replicant mage could maintain some wisdom, although I imagine such a mage would be more interested in making imbued items out of their mechanical parts then in mage politics. If the mage wasn't already a legacy member, a new one will certainly be in the making.<br /> <br /> Promethean- How many unfleshed centimanni are really of another lineage? Becoming human would certainly be the last thing on a replicant's mind.<br /> <br /> Hunter- A replicant hunter that keeps to the vigil would be interesting, although most would fall into the slasher trap most likely. Of course the rumors of Cheiron having replicants on staff are entirely false. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Mummy- Congratulations, you've got a necron. A replicant mummy would remain low on memory one would think, which would mean they remain more automaton-like.<br /> <br /> Demon- That is a hard one, demons are already machines, would the replicant curse only apply to a single cover? If it extends to all covers, well becoming a robotic hermit with an intense loathing for humanity is really not going to helpful to maintaining most covers, leaving such a demon in for a hard time. <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-08T14:21:39-07:00","id":910319,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the tips:<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_PjeczJEwC8\" target=\"_blank\">Continuing;</a><br /> <br /> <b>Liberation From Flesh:</b><br /> <br /> Part of the horror factor when it comes to fabricants is their viral nature-the realization they aren't truly replacing <i>anyone, </i>but infecting them with their hatred. Many players might be interested in roleplaying the metamorphosis, either completely or partially before fighting off the encroaching rage and inhuman body. Thus, it is useful to have a system for the process of &quot;liberation,&quot; especially if a character's friendly Contacts (or even a True Friend, if the player permits it) are turned and wish to bring their friend into the purity of metallic existence, for their own comfort. Please keep in mind that, even if a PC is cured, the complete prejudice futurians instinctively feel towards humans and anything else with more hydrocarbons than iron is a massive shift in characterization, and one many players are not comfortable with, on either end (ask any D&amp;D party with a player that could be titled &quot;Chaotic Stupid&quot;). Clear this with your players first.<br /> <br /> <b>Fabrication (Persistent, Tilt): </b><br /> <br /> This organic character has been touched by whatever hate-powered forces that turn animal matter into mechanical frames, and it is quietly pushing him or her towards misanthropy and only valuing the imperishable nature of machines. Organic characters (including undead ones) who interact with your character in a meaningful way (a conversation that goes beyond small talk, an argument, or a vampire feeding on their blood are all good barometers) get a reflexive Manipulation + Empathy - the character's Composure to detect that there is something &quot;off&quot; about this character's emotions (they won't necessarily realize this is because of growing disgust with them unless they score an exceptional success, but they can tell there's an unnatural and likely deliberate coldness in their demeanor). In addition, the character gains one of the list of traits below, as their body becomes an advanced machine (all Attribute enhancements can take them above 5, though not Skills):<br /> <br /> * Adaptive Musculature: The character gains +2 Dexterity and +2 Athletics, which can take them above 5.<br /> <br /> * Cognitive Programming: The character gains +2 Intelligence, which can take them above 5, and the Good Time Management Merit regardless of prerequisites<br /> <br /> * Redundant Systems: The character no longer suffers wound penalties, nor needs to roll to remain conscious when the rightmost Health box is full.<br /> <br /> * Repair Protocols: The character gains +3 Science and a Specialty in Engineering, and +3 Computer with a Specialty in Adapting Obsolete Technology<br /> <br /> * Resistant Materials: Natural armor 2B/2L, which stacks with any the character is wearing.<br /> <br /> <b>Beat: </b>Whenever the character's growing hatred of organic life causes long-term problems. This also results in a cumulative -1 penalty to the next &quot;progression&quot; roll, below<br /> <br /> <b>Resolution: </b>Transformation into a fabricant, while it <i>apparently </i>doesn't have a germ (or more likely nanobot) component (there hasn't been much opportunities for research on the project), is still a disease in many ways-it progresses gradually, and can be fought off if resisted. When a character gains this Tilt, note whether they are a &quot;progenitor&quot; infection (got the infection from being outside time, and thus will be the first of a new code if they are fully fabricated) or a &quot;secondary&quot; (got it from another fabricant). Once a month for progenitors and once an hour for secondaries (barring some arcane process of deliberately slowing the infection for the latter, which is not infrequent-it allows for greater subtlety), the character rolls Resolve + Integrity (or equivalent advantage, if they have it) + Supernatural Advantage (if present) vs. the number of seconds they were outside time (progenitors) or as a Clash of Wills against the fabricant that caused the Tilt (for secondaries). Failure on this roll means the infection progresses-stack another instance of the Tilt. Every stack beyond the initial gives the roll to detect something awry with the nascent fabricant a +1 bonus, and every second stack gives the character another fabricant trait. After three stacks, the character's converted digested system loses the ability to break down meat (the alcohols needed for biofuel aren't easily refined from animal matter), forcing a strictly vegan diet, though the character needs to only check once every three days for starvation (they still need to use the restroom, but to only vent liquid waste product that wasn't converted). At five stacks, natural healing ceases, instead forcing a Dexterity + Crafts roll to fix damage, which does not need proper tools unless the damage is aggravated (even a proto-fabricant without Repair Protocols instinctively understands enough about their new body to know how to jury-rig a repair), which takes five seconds for bashing damage, five minutes for lethal and five hours for aggravated, each success healing one box of Health. At seven, their eyes become mechanical optics, and a called shot to the head (even bashing damage) will damage them-the character still sees perfectly well, but the image of human eyes become broken up and staticky, impossible to mistake for anything natural, until the damage is repaired. More failures than the character's Resolve + Composure means the character has hit the point of no return; in a few short minutes, they develop any weaknesses and abilities they have not already, the skin develops the needed tailoring to be taken on and off, and if mortal develops the Dread Powers of their code; the Tilt is resolved as the character has been fully transformed into a new technogore. Success means the infection stalls-five over the course of the Tilt breaks the infection, and the character loses one stack of Fabrication every day until the Tilt is fully lost.<br /> <br /> Possible bonuses: The character has saved an human's life before the roll (+5), the character has regained Willpower from their Virtue in the past week (+3), Merits that depend on empathy and friendship with organics (+1 for every dot), Teamwork actions to remind the character of compassion for organic life (varies)<br /> <br /> Possible penalties: The character murders a human (-5), the character deals with the stress by retreating from the world completely (-3), the character exploits the fabricant traits for violent actions (-2), the character has gained a Beat from this Tilt before the next roll (-1, cumulative), the fabricant that caused this condition knows the character well (-2, secondaries only).<br /> <br /> <b>Special: Supernatural Technogores</b><br /> <br /> The fabrication process isn't choosy; so long as the infected subject is a mostly organic life form with a nature that does not directly conflict with the robotic nature of the being he is becoming, the infection can take root and possibly complete metamorphosis. This means that creatures actually <i>immune </i>to fabrication is an incredibly narrow category. Besides the infamous tendency of fabricants to convert animals into lethal robotic minions and pets, this also means the monsters of the world are quite capable of being incorporated into the code as powerful living weapons against animal life. While they don't replicate a code's Dread Powers, this is only because the supernatural components of a fabricant body are dedicated to replicating organic features of a monstrous fabricant's abilities; vampiric fangs are easily swapped for hypodermic needles, a Promethean's ability to feed on energy to heal is simply given a set of power collectors for the same purpose, a werewolf's shapeshifiting can easily be to a frame with a different set of weapons and propulsion system (and the wolf-people are an especially juicy prize-just because <i>natural </i>healing isn't possible doesn't mean <i>regeneration </i>isn't, wires and gears reassembling themselves and reattaching even as they are dented).<br /> <br /> About the only published (at the time of this writing) monsters completely immune to fabrication are Unfleshed Prometheans (who are already living machines) and the Unchained (who are also living machines, except for their Covers-which treat the encroaching fabrication as hostile malware). Being outside time does not expose them to the infection, and all feeding them fabricant flesh does is make them very sick, and in the case of beings capable of sleep, give them terrible nightmares of a mechanized world where unconverted organics are treated to horrifying slavery in the halls of a robotic nobility (this is actually not any different from a futurian's normal wish fulfillment dream, but immunes lack the fundamental hostility that causes their minds to interpret it as anything other than a surreal horror). The monsters <i>partially </i>immune to infection are a little less narrow, but not by much, and it depends on having an odd relationship with time and its sibling, destiny; Changelings and Arisen, along with whatever others have temporal manipulation fundamental to their beings (ST's discretion; it should not just be beings with the power to manipulate time, but for whom causality is tilted by the very nature of their existence-if <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/779860-siren-the-surfacing-nwod-abandoned-fangame-resurrection\">Sirens</a> exist in your CofD, the fact they they themselves are products of a potential future and are born to pull destiny away from that future are a good example). The infection takes with them, but it stalls out at three stacks (when the Fabricated individual must eat only plant matter), and the aggression never goes into outright hatred; the partial immune may become extremely snappy and impatient with organics, but apart from transhumanist sympathies, this never quite goes into disgust for organic life as a concept (and in fact, the partial immune may not realize anything's wrong until someone actually shows them the mechanical bits covering their insides). Ironically, this also means the partially immunes are also the ones that others may notice acting strange, since the emotions of anger are actually a lot harder to conceal than hatred. In game terms, the Beat Condition for partially is changed to &quot;gain a Beat whenever you act against the wishes of organic allies to protect them from their fundamental weaknesses&quot;, and at three stacks, failing roll to resist further infection means the next roll will be after a three month interval-the fabrication halts, but gets more entrenched. They never become full fabircants.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, there are one thing that make this not nearly as appealing as it could be; the fundamental hostility that fabricants feel towards organic beings is simply <i>not present </i>for immunes. This isn't a particularly special sense that alerts them to something being off when they realize they <i>aren't </i>disgusted towards this seemingly normal individual, it's that when they find out, the ire...vanishes. For Unfleshed and Unchained, the reasons should be obvious, but as soon as they realize otherwise, the aggression rapidly tapers off. For partial immunes, the anger is still present, but even then, it only goes into feelings of irritation and annoyance; more patronizing than actually vicious. Why is unknown; is it because the programming of the infection realizes it's pointless? Is it because none of these creatures really obeys the normal nature of hydrocarbon life?<br /> <br /> Or-as many more paranoid scholars think-is it because all of the above have beings who can be won over to the fabricants' cause through reasoned argument?<br /> <br /> Ephemerals, as a whole, do not naturally care as a whole if the dominant species is a mechanical one-the futurians' emotions generate Resonance and contribute to the Astral same as any other human, ghosts are the very definition of provincial and don't care unless the liberation affects their personal goals, and angels are, well, angels. It depends on what the God-Machine feels is best at the time. Unfleshed, like most other Prometheans, have a Centimani contingent, and discovering there's a race of humans who have finally clued into how disgusting it is as a species (and doesn't naturally feel Disquiet-<i>all </i>instinctive aggression is gone) thrills no small part of them. Unchained have no small number of misanthropes themselves who view humans as mindless cogs in Infrastructure anyway, and converting the Machine's tools into allies appeals to them (and some Integrators may think they're doing what God wants and making a new race of man in Its image). Partial immunes less so, but just because the fabrication process stalls doesn't mean they're immune to mental influence; so long as a fabricant can phrase their mission in such a way the infected carrier believes what they're doing is not fundamentally wrong (that fabricants made of iron are natural enemies of the True Fae, that they cannot sin against a particular Judge, that they put far less strain on the environment due to a lack of need for meat or as much for crops), any Social Influence for the fabricant to convince the carrier to help automatically succeeds at the roll for a good first impression, and with the carrier having one open Door.<br /> <br /> Thus, the immunes unwillingly trade the terror of being assimilated into something hostile and alien for a double dose for fear of betrayal; being a member of an immune species simply means any conspirators with the fabricants cannot have their allegiance revealed by tearing skin.<br /> <br /> (more to come)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","user":"Leliel","date":". 06-10-2016, 10:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T03:13:31-07:00","id":911549,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure about changelings being immune, sure, their mien is presented as the truth, while their mask is presented as a lie, but unless things change in second edition the changeling is also 100% biologically human.<br /> <br /> So, sure, you're changeling character might be, say, a rock, and that's true, but on the other hand, you're a rock that can give blood, catch a cold, grow old, leave DNA evidence, get high thanks to smoking a joint, and just maybe father or mother a child. <br /> <br /> Or did I gloss over a preview of changeling second edition that definitely says that changelings aren't biologically human anymore?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T05:48:03-07:00","id":911591,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>StSword</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=911549#post911549\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I'm not sure about changelings being immune, sure, their mien is presented as the truth, while their mask is presented as a lie, but unless things change in second edition the changeling is also 100% biologically human.<br /> <br /> So, sure, you're changeling character might be, say, a rock, and that's true, but on the other hand, you're a rock that can give blood, catch a cold, grow old, leave DNA evidence, get high thanks to smoking a joint, and just maybe father or mother a child.<br /> <br /> Or did I gloss over a preview of changeling second edition that definitely says that changelings aren't biologically human anymore?</div> </div> </div> </div>You didn't. I added that in to raise questions of how far changelings are from human (have they been so altered that even their <i>biochemical makeup </i>is more based on dream and Wyrd rather than biological compounds?), because of their mutually antagonistic relationship with iron (that's actually more key to their immunity) and because, as mentioned, changelings have a culture that could ally with fabricants all on their own, without being converted first. It helps tie into the natural paranoia of the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T06:16:32-07:00","id":911605,"title":"","content":"Changelings aren't biologically human, even in 2e. I don't recall an exact line that says that, but I never got that impression from the preview. After all, they can drop the Mask to reveal how they really are, which is human-based, but warped into something else. They're still partially human, but being able to bleed, have DNA, make babies, etc. is just as possible with, say, werewolves, who are also not human. The only evidence I can think of offhand comes from Beast, where Changelings are close kin (like werewolves, vampires, etc.) rather than distant kin (like mages, minor templates, etc.) or non-kin (like demons and humans). <br /> <br /> I think it's neat that Changelings are immune, and fits thematically with how they work. However, I might argue that they, of the immune groups, might still trigger the hatred of organics, because while they are Wyrd-infused, they have a basis of flesh (same as non-Unfleshed Prometheans). Also, infecting Changelings might count as hitting them with cold iron? Maybe not; cold iron is supposed to be pretty rare, and wouldn't make sense from, for example, a crystalline fabricant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T06:31:40-07:00","id":911612,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=911605#post911605\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Changelings aren't biologically human, even in 2e. I don't recall an exact line that says that, but I never got that impression from the preview. After all, they can drop the Mask to reveal how they really are, which is human-based, but warped into something else. They're still partially human, but being able to bleed, have DNA, make babies, etc. is just as possible with, say, werewolves, who are also not human. The only evidence I can think of offhand comes from Beast, where Changelings are close kin (like werewolves, vampires, etc.) rather than distant kin (like mages, minor templates, etc.) or non-kin (like demons and humans).<br /> <br /> I think it's neat that Changelings are immune, and fits thematically with how they work. However, I might argue that they, of the immune groups, might still trigger the hatred of organics, because while they are Wyrd-infused, they have a basis of flesh (same as non-Unfleshed Prometheans). Also, infecting Changelings might count as hitting them with cold iron? Maybe not; cold iron is supposed to be pretty rare, and wouldn't make sense from, for example, a crystalline fabricant.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Okay, that's true. I'll alter it a bit to be &quot;partially immune&quot; (because of the timelessness).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T07:14:05-07:00","id":911630,"title":"","content":"Okay, I created a &quot;partially immune carrier&quot; version. Which now means Arisen themselves cannot become Necrons, but Sadikh and their cults? Wonders.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T07:33:22-07:00","id":911644,"title":"","content":"Question: are Acanthus Mages (Fate and Time) partially immune? If so, what about Mages who know the Arcana Fate and Time?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-10T10:35:29-07:00","id":911754,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=911644#post911644\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Question: are Acanthus Mages (Fate and Time) partially immune? If so, what about Mages who know the Arcana Fate and Time?</div> </div> </div> </div>I was tempted to do that, but no-Acanthus aren't inherently divorced from time's normal flow, they need magic to do that.<br /> <br /> Good plot hook for those who have actually done that, though-I'll put that in the Storytelling section.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-12T07:31:49-07:00","id":912788,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>AceOfAlmonds</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/782555-gurps-monster-conversions?p=911605#post911605\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Changelings aren't biologically human, even in 2e. I don't recall an exact line that says that, but I never got that impression from the preview. After all, they can drop the Mask to reveal how they really are, which is human-based, but warped into something else. They're still partially human, but being able to bleed, have DNA, make babies, etc. is just as possible with, say, werewolves, who are also not human. The only evidence I can think of offhand comes from Beast, where Changelings are close kin (like werewolves, vampires, etc.) rather than distant kin (like mages, minor templates, etc.) or non-kin (like demons and humans).</div> </div> </div> </div>Werewolves and changelings are apples and oranges.<br /> <br /> When werewolves are in human form they have human DNA. When werewolves are in wolf form they have wolf DNA. When they are in hybrid form they have crazy DNA. When changelings have their mask up they have human DNA. When they drop the mask they still have human DNA.<br /> <br /> When werewolves are in human form they respond to drugs and poisons as a human. When they are in wolf form they respond to drugs and poisons as a wolf does. When a werewolf is in hybrid forms they respond in crazy mashup fashion. When a changeling has their mask up they respond to drugs and poisons as a human does. When a changeling drops the mask they still respond to drugs and poisons as a human would.<br /> <br /> When a werewolf changes into a wolf they're a wolf, when a changeling drops the mask to reveal they are a rock they aren't actually a rock, or they wouldn't have DNA, or blood, or an immune system that worries about the flu, or responds to chemicals just like a human body does. In point of fact, a changeling can't even weigh as much as a rock of their size, the mask and mien can only differ by 20 pounds if memory serves. A granite statue of a 200 lb man would weigh 550 lbs if made out of granite, 520 lbs if marble, the changeling is stuck weighing 180 to 220 lbs in their mien if their mask is 200 lbs. <br /> <br /> And looking to changeling's crossover rules, there's the fact that vampires can drink the blood of changelings. Vampires can only drink blood. If a changeling didn't have blood but oil because they were a machine, water because they're actually made from ice, or sap because they're really a tree, then the vampire wouldn't be able to drink their nonexistent blood, now would they?<br /> <br /> Also, in the crossover section dealing with werewolves, it stated that werewolves can eat changelings to replenish essence. Eating to replenish essence only works with humans, wolves, and spirits. Changelings certainly don't count as wolves or spirits, I'm pretty sure. So unless the writer and author completely spaced out, the natural conclusion is that they considered changelings to be human. Of course it's possible they did completely space out, since in werewolf first edition it was explicitly stated that werewolves couldn't eat other supernaturals for essence, so either they forgot, or they disagreed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":391,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","user":"StSword","date":". 06-12-2016, 10:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-15T14:47:38-07:00","id":915100,"title":"","content":"<b>Example Characters</b><br /> <br /> <i>Concealed Guard Dog</i><br /> <br /> <b>Quote</b>: <i>*ominous hum* bzzzrrr...ruff! Ruff!</i><br /> <br /> This pooch wasn't originally a guard dog. In fact, it isn't even a good breed for being one; it's a Pomeranian, which are very much more bark than bite, especially when it comes to humans. Then its master was fully fabricated, and loyalty to his pet won out over his newfound disgust for non-polymer fluff. A cubic inch of futurian skin in its kibble later, the problem was solved-and then some, giving the fabricant pom a set of lethal claws and a taser at the back of its throat. Now, both master and pet stalk through the sidewalks, colder than any normal human or toy breed, perversely hoping some foolish organic attacks them. Then they have an excuse to demonstrate <i>exactly </i>how ruthlessly efficient the dog is at removing threats.<br /> <br /> <b>Description: </b>In daily life, the Concealed Guard Dog looks like most other Pomeranians, a hyperactive ball of tan fluff with fur twice as long as the actual dog. To people on the street, it comes off as a bit growly, but not more so than any other grouchy canid (the native intelligence of fabricants allowed its owner to train it from assaulting passerby on sight very easily). When relaxed or attacking, though, the fur falls off in a fluid motion, likely even as the technogore pet is launching itself at a threat's throat. What's under the fur? A skinny, skeletal nightmare of plastics and hydraulics, more insect than mammal, with four extendable ceramic claws perfect for quick laceration of flesh, and a mouth sparking with blue electricity-a gateway into a technological Hell.<br /> <br /> <b>Stoytelling Hints: </b>Fabricated animals like the Guard Dog are substantially more intelligent than their organic versions, and thus significantly more trainable. Indeed, to other fabricants, they're actually incredibly friendly-they can't eat meat any more and they know it, so much of their predation and territorial instincts are lost-in relation to other technogores. To <i>anything else, </i>even members of their former species, the fabricant makes a cold calculation of whether it it prudent to kill them now or seem nonthreatening enough to be ignored until the time the organic can be killed. Trained ones generally serve as their human fabricant masters' scouts and disposable minions. The Guard Dog's owner values his own pet too much to view it as a not-meat shield, but it serves a similar function; it's small and cunning enough to slip everywhere there's a large enough hole, and from there retrieve anything it can fit in its mouth and tear apart a target or too (never direct sabotage, though-fabricants hate destroying complex machinery). At its core though, it is a guardian-one capable of picking off intruders in its home one by one while pretending to be a perfectly normal dog, or serving as a merciless tag team with its owner.<br /> <br /> <b>Attributes</b>: Intelligence 3, Wits 4, Resolve 3,<br /> Strength 4, Dexterity 6, Stamina 3, Presence 3,<br /> Manipulation 0, Composure 1<br /> <br /> <b>General Dice Pools: </b>Tracker 8, Stealth 8, Fitting Through Holes 7, Seeming Harmless 6, Unnerving Synthesized Growl 6<br /> <br /> <b>Combat Dice Pools: </b>Ambush 7, Scything Claws 6, Jaw Taser 6*<br /> <br /> * Inflicts bashing damage, but a hit inflicts a debilitating tilt like Stunned or Arm Wrack<br /> <br /> <b>Initiative Modifier</b>: +5<br /> <br /> <b>Defense</b>: 7<br /> <br /> <b>Health</b>: 7<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower</b>: 4<br /> <br /> <b>Size</b>: 4<br /> <br /> <b>Speed:</b> 12<br /> <br /> <b>Dread Powers</b>: Natural Weapons **, Prodigious Leap, Wall Climb <br /> <br /> <b>Ban</b>: Like all fabricants, the Concealed Guard Dog's Dread Powers are hazardous to its skin-using them without slipping out first (which is an instant action to take off and takes two turns to get on). If it uses them while still in skin, the semi-carbon covering will tear and the fabricant will be exposed for the hour it takes to regenerate.<br /> <br /> The Concealed Guard Dog has all the enhancements outlined in the Fabrication condition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Lindsey Axle, Cybernetic Diva</i><br /> <br /> <b>Quotes: </b><i>(public)</i> <i>Yes, I</i> have <i>aged well! Wanna know my secret? </i><br /> <br /> <i>(revealed) Stop staring. I don't care if you're horrified or aroused, junk-you won't have those feelings soon enough. Good fucking riddance.</i><br /> <br /> <b>Background: </b>Linda Randal's life has been a series of &quot;nearly&quot;. She nearly wasn't born, but the contraceptives her mother used had expired and proved ineffectual. She nearly had a good parent, but the judge overseeing her custody hearings was a homophobe who realized her &quot;aunt&quot; was her mom's actual lover. She nearly got a good enough grade to graduate on a scholarship, then her neglectful drunk of a dad had to sell their shared car to pay off a loan shark on final exam day. She nearly got her dream of becoming the next pop idol, but a jealous competitor replaced her makeup with an acidic compound, and she couldn't pay for the corrective surgery to make herself photogenic.<br /> <br /> She nearly avoided being selected for the secret experiment into temporal manipulation that turned her into a far colder being than any celebrity ice queen, but she ranted about her life to the overseer of the project, which convinced him she was a worthy charity case for his ideas about how to &quot;edit&quot; a person's personal timeline for medical purposes. It was just skin, he reasoned-easy enough to fix if something went wrong.<br /> <br /> Nothing went wrong with the <i>procedure, </i>and Linda nailed it on the second tryout. However, the very premise behind that machine meant that she was outside of time for five seconds while the edits took effect-more than enough time for a moderate fabrication infection to root itself in her body. She nearly was the exact kind of person who could fight it off, except by this point, the newly dubbed Lindsey Axel had long developed a very, <i>very </i>deep anger with the world-fueling that into hatred of the irrational, vicious organics that had made her life a living hell was something she was already doing on a small scale. And nobody noticed-singers, after all, are actually expected to be rather grouchy and catty outside their public image, even if their lyrics take a turn for the bitterly cynical and brutal lambastings of human folly (really, the producers were more concerned she was &quot;too unique&quot;-a quick conversion of the PR department stopped that from slowing her down).<br /> <br /> Now the progenitor of a code of futurians based on telecommunications technology, Lindsey isn't the most famous singer in the world, but she's famous and frugal enough to be considered rich (she doesn't need a lot of the creature comforts, after all). While she's primarily known only to music fans for her return to classic techno/rap with a fashionable steampunk aesthetic, music fans are a pretty large group. Really, many people think she could be more famous, if she really wanted to market herself. In fact, she's doing that deliberately-less fame means less scandal-seeking paparazzi that could get documentation of something very <i>horrific </i>about her true identity. She actually likes her job and sings because she finds it fun and rewarding-and it's an excellent distribution network for her strain of the fabricant infection. She's responsible for no less than five cells of her code all over the United States, and she plans to take it global; Japan's a good bet, though she's partial to India (tech support firms are her code's natural home ground). At night, when she's packed up her skin and running REM cycles to refresh her mind, she does indeed dream of becoming more famous-but not as one idol among thousands. No, she fantasizes of being the first of a new, perfect, celebrity class, without age or addiction or vice. The idols and darlings of a new age of machines, their bodies being the most artificial things about them.<br /> <br /> In her dark moments, she admits to herself that, perhaps then, she won't feel so empty.<br /> <br /> <b>Description: </b>People often aren't sure what to make of Lindsey Axel when they meet her up close-especially without her makeup on. She's pretty, of course-the music industry wants videos, and you can't have <i>ugly </i>(or even normal-looking, or frequently <i>non-white</i>) people starring in TV. Heavens no, and let's be frank-Axel is beautiful, with her finely toned swimmer's body and intense green eyes and sense of just how to accent her clothes with enough pseudo-Victorian and clockwork wear to comes off as unique rather than tacky. But the thing is, it never stops-once the makeup comes off, one can see there's actual signs that someone was born rather than constructed. Not Lindsey; she looks like she's always been dolled up for a photoshoot, though not quite symmetrical-she lacks most imperfections beyond some freckles. Also, the fact that her hair's almost always blue-eventually people wonder if she ever gets sick of the dye.<br /> <br /> In fact, that blue's her &quot;natural&quot; shade-and it's not really hair, either, but hundreds of cables and antennae her body manufactures over the course of her mechanized life. Under her skin, she's still beautiful, but inhuman-a slender, mostly androgynous android made of millions of intertwined wires, kept safely in place by pristine black plates of polymer and steel, occasionally broken up by bits of other technology that she's inserted into her form to make herself stronger-a smartphone here, the insides of a supercomputer there. She has a face composed of over a hundred of these tiny plates that easily shift to give her the full capacity of human expression. If anyone survived unconverted, they might call her form &quot;HR Giger's idea of a supermodel&quot;, an idea only furthered when she literally merges bits of herself with surrounding technology to better control and manipulate it. Disturbingly, her eyes remain perfectly normal, up until the point she needs her Hypnotic Gaze-then they flash with a dizzying array of patterns that effectively send whoever she's locking gaze with into a state of ecstasy-many of her followers see her as more divine than she really thinks of herself as for that exact reason.<br /> <br /> <b>Storytelling Hints: </b>Lindsey long believed the vast majority of people were garbage long before she became a fabricant-she may have only come up with the insult &quot;junk&quot; after she became a far more realistic (and physical) singing robot than any Vocaloid, but really, most of what she saw of humanity had long convinced her most of the species was a pointless drain on the Earth's resources. If she's trying to recruit someone to her cause, she makes a large point about the environmental benefits of having a society that does not need as nearly as much to eat or overgraze with more animal types of food, and to be fair, she believes what she's selling-but really, she's the first to admit one of her major motives is returning some of the suffering she's been put through by <i>homo sapiens </i>tenfold as their immortal slavemistress (while liberating the people she likes, of course). Every song she makes <i>oozes </i>with her fabrication-enhanced resentment of the dominant species, and it's clear to anyone who meets her outside of the mask of the cheerful but rebellious counterculture idol or the apathetic and perfectionist diva; while some fabricants are cold and resolute in their hatred of organics, Axle rants and raves, peppering her speech with regular insults aimed at the human condition and affirmation of the idea that organic life was a mistake barely capable of giving rise to its solution. In particular, she hates the way she, by virtue of being female and a celebrity, is automatically a sex object; she was asexual and aromantic before her fabrication, and being turned into a machine that literally cannot reproduce has deepened her estrangement with sex and love into outright condemnation of the concept. To her, anything romantic is simply a dominance game between two hormone-driven, borderline mindless beasts desperately attempting to prove power over the other.<br /> <br /> Still, she's not a happy woman. She wasn't ever, but at one point, she honestly believed that liberating the few allies (she doesn't really have &quot;friends&quot;) she had and divesting them of petty hungers she despised would give her life something resembling a purpose. As it turns out, people don't work like that; a shallow, superficial person becomes a shallow, superficial fabricant; it has nothing to do with hormones (or if it does, whatever arcane designs are behind fabricants feel they're key enough to a full personality they are simulated perfectly). In fact, she hasn't even fabricated the band members for precisely that reason-she's given up hope of actually making them into people she can tolerate on a personal basis, and on a purely practical level doesn't trust them to not wave their newly-mechanical dongs around in a way that will expose them to actual threats. She doesn't have the same malice towards the fans who like her purely for the music, but the rigors of tour life mean she can't actually socialize with her secondary cells at all (not to mention she sometimes has to kill those that the infection didn't take in. She hates that). And so, Lindsey throws herself into her job, distracting herself from the misery of her existence with perfectionism-both as a songwriter and as a mastermind behind her code. She won't ever stop, though-she's had enough of &quot;nearly&quot; to settle for &quot;nearly won.&quot;<br /> <br /> Fabrication Conditions are already figured into the below statblock. Keep in mind Lindsey is the progenitor of her entire code, and has had the longest time and most resources to upgrade herself; she's substantially stronger than most futurians just after complete fabrication. Her code's defining Dread Powers are her Eye Spy, Influence (though most only have one or two Dots of Influence), and Gremlin, while her Ban is shared by all of them.<br /> <br /> <b>Mental Attributes:</b> Intelligence 4, Wits 5, Resolve 4<br /> <br /> <b>Physical Attributes: </b>Strength 3, Dexterity 5, Stamina 3 *<br /> <br /> <b>Social Attributes</b>: Presence 5, Manipulation 7, Composure 6<br /> <br /> * Lindsey Axle is a machine, but like other fabricants (or mundane robots, for that matter), there's only so far she can push her circuits before they start to wear out. She doesn't need to roll to resist toxins or remain conscious when tired, but it does figure into her Health and all other rolls.<br /> <br /> <b>Mental Skills</b>: Academics 1 Computer 1, Crafts 3 (Adapting Obsolete Technology), Investigation 1, Occult 1, Science 3 (Engineering), Politics 3 (Media Office), <br /> <br /> <b>Physical Skills</b>: Athletics 2, Brawl 1, Larceny 1, Stealth 2 <br /> <br /> <b>Social Skills: </b> Expression 4 (Songwriting), Intimidation 1, Persuasion 3, Streetwise 2, Subterfuge 2 (Mask for the Public), Socialize 2<br /> <br /> <b>Merits:</b> Contacts ** (Her Converted Followers and Media) Fame **, Good Time Management, Pusher, Resources ***, Status (Music Industry) **, Striking Looks *, <br /> <br /> <b>Potency: </b>4<br /> <br /> <b>Willpower: </b>14<br /> <br /> <b>Virtue: </b>Diligence-Lindsey is the best kind of perfectionist, one who isn't paralyzed by flaws or problems and in fact regards them as indications she needs to try smarter. Whether it be lyrics or her diabolical schemes to one day conquer the planet with her fabricant followers.<br /> <br /> <b>Vice:</b> Vindictive-She's not one to let grudges go. Before she was fabricated, this was a source of self-loathing, but now? Now she takes it as an ever-growing list of reasons to not just <i>defeat </i>organics, but <i>punish </i>them.<br /> <br /> <b>Aspirations: </b>Extend her influence across the world, Protect her true nature from discovery, Maintain career and fame.<br /> <br /> <b>Initiative: </b>11<br /> <br /> <b>Defense: </b>7<br /> <br /> <b>Speed: </b>13<br /> <br /> <b>Health: </b>8<br /> <br /> <b>Ban: </b>Like most fabricants, Lindsey has Dread Powers that cannot be used without tearing her skin and revealing the true horror within; without taking off her skin (which is an instant action to take off and takes two turns to get on), Lindsey's Eye Spy and Influence Dread Powers damage it, leaving her exposed for the hour or so it takes to regenerate. Why is due to the way those powers function-she must be in contact with the device she plans to use or a different device connected to it on a network to use her powers, as her antennae hair and wires from under her skeletal plating reach out and merge with the device via arcane jacks and electrical pads, connecting her internal network to that of the system. This hints at how her code of the fabricant infection can be cured-by uploading a virus meant to subsume a computer to a botnet (a distributed network under the control of the owner's computer, generally used for spam emails, DDOS attacks, and brute force decryption), one can briefly restore the capacity to feel empathy with organic life to the fabricant-from there, one can use the opportunity to invoke Social Maneuvering with the goal of getting the fabricant to understand there's something worthwhile about organic life, with success meaning the fabricant returns to their normal, organic self over the course of a month.<br /> <br /> <b>Dread Powers: </b>Eye Spy (Anything Digital That Can See), Gremlin, Hypnotic Gaze, Influence *** (Computers and Computer-Operated Devices)<br /> <br /> (will continue)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T02:55:30-07:00","id":917268,"title":"","content":"Oh, Leliel, I had a thought, since you established ephemerals as being immune to the fabrication curse, what about ephemeral based beings that actually have bodies?<br /> <br /> A claimed, revenant, or bhuta gets unstuck in time, what happens?<br /> <br /> I figure being dead bodies possessed by ghosts, that revenants and bhuta, the answer would be &quot;nothing.&quot; As much as I happen to like the idea of a mechanical revenant. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Claimed are the tricky ones, would nothing happen, or would they become fabricants with the spirit in charge as normal?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-19T10:03:07-07:00","id":917408,"title":"","content":"Hmm...<br /> <br /> I'll post my answer in the Werewolf crossover section.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-04T14:00:07-07:00","id":963375,"title":"","content":"Well, now that my life is sane, how about I continue this?<br /> <br /> <b>Storytelling Hints: </b>Fabricant stories are invasion stories. On the macro level, it's about the gradual replacement of the familiar with a hostile force that hates everything about you for the crime of existing, the transformation of the safe world into enemy ground, and not seeing it until it is far too late. The infection assimilates its enemies and transforms everyone with an inner darkness or a weak will into an asset, and the paranoia it engenders too often leads to distrust of humanity-and from there, the very hate the infection feeds on. The invasion works on a personal level too, if the players are infected-an unknown force gradually infiltrates their very chemical makeup and turns them into something unrecognizable as having ever been an organic being, and transforms their mind into something horrifying. Even immunes and partially immunes aren't exempt from this-for partial immunes, the infection is still dancing through their minds, making working with the fabricants seem reasonable despite their utter hatred of everything from Kingdom Animalia.<br /> <br /> There's a more subtle theme in there than just the viral robots taking over the town and then (in their wildest dreams) the world, though-dehumanization. From the perspective of the technogores, it's the growing separation from the species they once were and life in general until they feel that all else must be either suborned or destroyed to give them peace of mind. To them, the world stops being &quot;real&quot;, only an object of disgust. For the uninfected around them, the fabricants become steadily more inhuman by themselves, physically and mentally. Ultimately, both human and futurian become mutually exclusive beings, creatures that desire naught but the complete extermination of the other without pause or thought about who the others are as people. Of course, in most stories about mutual xenophobia, one species is not biologically (in a manner of speaking) predisposed to eliminating the other, but ironically it can solved the same way as those more idealistic stories. Whether anyone will have the chance or idea before they're forced to violently decommission their friends and family, though...<br /> <br /> Gamelines that are well-suited to a fabricant story are:<br /> <br /> <b>Demon: </b>Unchained are no strangers to paranoia-many of them are even the cause. An invasion of fabricants is actually something of a relief-it's easy enough to tell when someone has been fully fabricated, you just cut the skin. That's usually when they discover that there's no small amount of sympathetic ears among them; as mentioned in the sidebar, many Unchained are already misanthropic enough to view the transformation from human to coldly ruthless futurian as an upgrade. That doesn't change the fact that Unchained are immune, which means they're also the ones who can solve the problem most safely-perhaps the most of all, because they can tell when flesh is infected without being fabricated themselves (they grow very sick while the Cover rejects the fabrication). Of course, the other question demons find themselves asking is whether fabricants are linked to another kind of often human-seeming race of machines they are intimately familiar with. On the one hand, fabricants are indeed built on principles that look more than advanced enough to qualify for occult physics. On the other, the rabidity and desire to change the world into an incredibly blatant mechanical wonderland certainly isn't exactly in the SOP of the God-Machine. Of course, the God-Machine is known to deviate from SOP quite deliberately to keep the Unchained on their toes, and angels have been reported working for and against different codes (though curiously, if one code is declared an ally or enemy for one angel, it remains as such for all others-something a bit less occulted than what it normally does). Thus, whether fabricants are an evolution of the Machine's plans, a glitch, or something completely unrelated depends on the Unchained you ask-and their own allegiances are even more varied. Even a humanistic Tempter sees the benefit in having a pet genius hitman...<br /> <br /> <b>Promethean: </b>Besides the aforementioned immunity that Unfleshed (and Constructs of other Lineages) have to fabrication making it safer to study them up close, a code of fabricants is an extremely useful case study in humanity by its absence-in fact, more than one Mystic has let the infection progress unabated, so as to have an unparalleled look into the process of becoming more and less than human (occasionally, the Vitriol gained form the milestone is even enough to recoup the lost progress on the Pilgrimage). After learning what they can from the futurians, though, it's almost invariable that non-Centimani Created attempt to eradicate or cure them; besides the fact that they are a threat to their human friends simply by virtue of fabricant existence, there's something about the fact that fabricants abandon all the best traits of humanity (compassion, empathy, appreciation for beauty) in order to become such a &quot;perfect&quot; machine that many Prometheans find morally offensive. Their entire existence is based around preparing for the metamorphosis into humanity, and here comes along some contagious mental illness that turns its hosts into the worst kind of Centimanus? Many Created aren't sure if they are more envious or disgusted with the fabricants complacency and their contempt for the gift of humanity (especially since fabricants cannot reproduce except by infection-the fundamental proof of worthlessness as a life form of any kind, only a virus). It doesn't help that the vast majority of Prometheans are still vulnerable, resulting in a massive stalling on the Pilgrimage while the Created reconnects with humanity or embraces Flux-there are no cases of non-Centimani Promethean fabricants. Of course, those same Centimani tend to think that a fabricant is an example of a human who finally gets it-many major outbreaks of technogores has been caused by a code finding a sympathetic Freak who can use their Pandorans as vectors.<br /> <br /> <b>Vampire: </b>While the unliving metaphor of the Kindred is generally more with sex and addiction than disease, there's a reason many of the medieval myths viewed them as more of a walking contagion slowly wearing down the living than as individuals in their own right. The All-Night Society has rather more in common with fabricants than anyone should be comfortable with; they're a virulent strain of creature that infects humans, giving them great physical and mental ability, but gradually reshaping them into callous, cold, and lifeless monsters who only care about the intelligent fulfillment of their instinctive prerogative. The main difference is that for a vampire, the debasement into a ruthless Beast is something they are partially at fault for their monstrosity-their condition doesn't feed on hate, and it doesn't destroy their capacity for empathy with their prey. To their credit, they can see the similarities too, and while they don't necessarily cotton on to the parts where they come out behind. they do realize that the very existence of futurians is a threat to more than just their meal ticket. It's actually incredibly rare for a Covenant as a whole to think it might be a good plan to weaponize the infection (even the Ordo Dracul thinks trying to study them without quarantine is a proposition that loses more than it gains)-not the least because vampires are not immune, and for them, it tends to be outright irreversible; Humanity is a measurement of inherent empathy to a degree, and fabrication is a mutually exclusive proposition with it. Quite simply, vampire fabricants are almost invariably dragur-and given the native intelligence and focus of fabricants, they're generally careful predators too.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-05T13:21:19-07:00","id":963874,"title":"","content":"Seeing as how Deionscribe isn't here right now, let's start up the next round of voting!<br /> <br /> <b>Corpse-Kisser: </b>Parasites are often a remarkable group of creatures. From <i>Toxoplasma gondii, </i>the protist that controls the minds of rats to make them feed themselves to cats where it can sexually reproduce, to <i>Cymothoa exigua, </i>the tongue-eating louse that <i>replaces </i>the lost organ in fish and then happily nibbles on its hosts' food, the evolutionary strategies of a creature that needs to live in and off of other creatures to survive is almost beautiful in their variety and cleverness. The cryptid centipedes known as taxidermites are not really hazardous to the living, but their reproductive drive is even more remarkable; to the untrained eye, they bring back the dead. Of course, said mobile colonies, the corpse-kissers proper (so called because of the easiest way to transplant a colony) are not really the dead they resemble-but they're good at <i>pretending </i>otherwise, as the taxidemites possess a true hive mind, one that easily grows large enough to develop human-scale intelligence in a human body. Good thing for them, as humans are the only species that gathers so many potential hosts in one easily accessed place. At their core though, corpse-kissers are animals, and they aren't nearly vicious enough to make the majority of corpses themselves. Unfortunately, they're also trainable-and large colony-groups have very simple needs for the aspiring mastermind to fulfill...<br /> <br /> <b>Tomb Moths and Tombwalkers: </b>Temples and crypts need guardians to protect their sacred grounds, but what happens when those guardians die or otherwise vacate their duty? They need to be replaced, obviously-and what better creature to replace them than the very would-be robbers? Bred from the silkmoth for the express purpose of ensuring that no infidel would escape a most ironic fate, tomb moths are eusocial insects whose scale dust contains a toxin that mummifies intruders alive, condemning them to a horrible, starving death inside a desiccated body-only for the moths to weave silken wrappings laced with necromantic energies around their victim, reanimating her as an intelligent undead who retains most to all of their previous life's knowledge, with a compulsion to protect and maintain the very site that destroyed her-and a biological need to, as the only thing they can eat any more are the larvae of the tomb moths. Thus, by necessity the tombwalker becomes a morbid entomologist by necessity-and, if she feels the need for another site to have an eternal guardian, nothing says she can't leave some excess larva behind her...<br /> <br /> <b>Wither Puppets: </b>Grotesque mariontettes, wither puppets are bodies jerked around by an unseen creature on spider-web strings. Whatever personality they once had is long gone, silenced by whatever eldritch puppetmaster awaits at the other end of their strings-but that's not the bad part. The <i>bad </i>part is that they're contagious-and it's never one death equals one puppet, oh no. The name comes from the fact that, whatever limb they touch shrivels and becomes useless-and instills the subconscious knowledge that the limb can be replaced with another. It's always easier than it looks, to swap out the seemingly dead limb for another, healthy one...y'know, apart from the whole &quot;it has to be fresh&quot; business. And it's always a perfect match, changing to resemble whatever was lost-often, the new owner is so happy he doesn't realize it twitches involuntarily. So when the puppet comes back to wilt another limb, he replaces the limb again. And again, And again, until there's more transplants than original parts. That's when the strings appear, and another skein-kin joins the shuddering march.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-05T14:46:45-07:00","id":963920,"title":"","content":"I'll go with Corpse-Kissers.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3514-zealouschristian24","name":"ZealousChristian24","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1796"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-05T14:56:22-07:00","id":963928,"title":"","content":"Also corpse kissers","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-05T23:54:27-07:00","id":964119,"title":"","content":"Tomb Moths and Tombwalkers.<br /> <br /> On a sidenote, I'll put together some write-ups for some of the other splats interacting with Fabricants. <hr /><br /> <b>Beast: </b>Many Begotten who stumble upon knowledge of the Fabricants develop a great fear of it, especially once they have learned the changes it inflicts on them. The transformation of a Beast results in a creature that uses the bonds of Kinship and the Chambers of its Lair as a vector for the Fabricant Condition. This kind of technogore can quickly give rise to other futurians, especially those of the supernatural variety. And even the Heroes created in the wake of their feedings (and, worse, their sibling Beasts) are not spared from their influence. Technogore Beasts infect other characters with the Fabrication Tilt through various means. Those who spend time in the Fabricant's Lair are affected as if they were progenitors. If the technogore has broodmates and/or has given the Family Ties Condition to other characters, it also affects them in this manner. All other interactions advance the transformation as if they were secondaries. In both cases, the Beast rolls for a Clash of Wills.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunter:</b><br /> <br /> <b>Changeling:<br /> <br /> Mage: </b>The Awakened who encounter futurians","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>\u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/806810-deionscribe-s-homebrew-hub#post806810\" target=\"_blank\">&quot;My Homebrew Hub&quot;</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\" target=\"_blank\">Age of Azar</a><br /> \u2022 <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/907224-the-kingdom-of-yamatai-dark-era\" target=\"_blank\">The Kingdom of Yamatai</a>","edited":{"userid":8180,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","user":"Deionscribe","date":". 09-06-2016, 10:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8180-deionscribe","name":"Deionscribe","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2265"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-09-08T19:06:14-07:00","id":965984,"title":"","content":"Seeing as how my normal timer is shot due to school, closing the vote.<br /> <br /> Corpse-kissers, here we come","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T20:30:20-08:00","id":1024611,"title":"","content":"Holy crap, has it really been four months?<br /> <br /> I can't juggle writing three things at once, it seems.<br /> <br /> Anyhoo;<br /> <br /> <b>Corpse-Kisser (Breeder, Hiveman)</b><br /> <br /> <i>Yes, yes, we get it. The dead-walker will be destroyed, and we will make the corpse our sister. Did you secure that entry to the morgue and fake IDs we asked? </i><br /> <br /> <br /> Nature has always put the lie to stating something is unnatural. &quot;You cannot turn a shattered animal into more animals.&quot; Sponges, when fed through a sieve, will recognize their cells and reform into smaller individuals. &quot;Single-celled organisms are microscopic.&quot; Plasmodial slime molds are multinuclear cells that expand rapidly to the point of being impossible to miss.<br /> <br /> &quot;Many organisms cannot be one ,individual.&quot; Besides cellular slime molds, which fuse into one body to spread their spores once they have depleted the local food source, the supernatural. parasitic arthropods known as taxidermites show the lie applies to Kingdom Animalia as well.<br /> <br /> Corpse-kissers are one part hive, one part adult imago of a complex lifecycle of a supernatural mutation of centipede. They are, in many ways, classic movie zombies; walking corpses puppeted by an eldritch force whose only motivations are survival, reproduction, and hunger, with all goals frequently being enfolded into one. Except they aren't zombies in the sense of being undead, unintelligent, or even a metaphor for disease; they're the preserved armor of a communal swarm of arthropods who simply found a combination food source, vehicle, and weapon against anything that seeks to eat them. Particularly in the sense of being mindless-while one taxidermite is about as intelligent as the bugs they resemble, each one's shell is covered in mystical patterns and compounds that, when many of them are linked together, function as neurons; in the corpse-kisser,their bodies transform into a gestalt brain of chitin, one that grows more complex and aware as the controlling colony lays asexual eggs and hatches new members-in two days, the hiveman is as intelligent as a chimpanzee. By the end of the week, the breeder can match the average human in creative thought, and speaks the local language fluently.<br /> <br /> In their base form, taxidermites resemble the centipedes they are a mutation of, moderately sized black bugs with large mandibles and oddly expressive eyes. They never let themselves be seen like this for long, not the least because they can't actually survive on their own for too long in adult form; the same enzymes they use to animate the corpse-kissers literally holds their exoskeleton together, and without the soup inside either a host or a protected nest, the taxidermites fall apart into a black, soot-like substance. Thus, to prevent drying, hivemen transfer colonies of taxidermites by pressing their mouth directly around an open orifice, hence their common appellation, as a body's mouth is the most reliable opening without damaging their children's new home. If no breeders are around to transfer adult corpse bugs, though, their gray-white nymph forms are far hardier and can afford to be clever about finding a host distant from any existing population-it just takes a while longer to mature once inside the host and from there create a colony capable of puppeteering the moribund hive.<br /> <br /> Once inside a host, taxidermites quickly embark on an eating spree, devouring every last trace of internal biology for biomass to give to genetically identical eggs that mature with supernatural speed. As they devour the biomass and secrete their enzymes, the taxidermite hive mind begins to form &quot;castes&quot; of a sort, each dedicated to a different area of controlling the body-limbs, bursts of speed, sight, balance, and so on. This is helped by the fact that while taxidermites cannot fully access their potential for intelligence until working in concert, their unique chitin does carry with it memories from host to host-as the colony grows, it develops a sort of &quot;genetic memory&quot; of how to control and imitate a normal member of the species the corpse-kisser was made from. It's for this reason that corpse-kissers in human bodies can speak the dominant language of their area, as the taxidermites realized that was probably essential to pass on to the young at some point in this era of urban living and textual instructions (hivemen try to avoid speaking if they can, however-there's only so far you can go to disguise the fact your voice is really a bunch of well-coordinated bugs vibrating intensely). While the bugs devour the eyes as well, the first item on their agenda of castes is to create ocular taxidermites that both report on what the body can see as well as are colored like normal eyes for their species, allowing them to pass as normal at first glance (they can't disguise the cracks where one curled bug ends and the next begins that well, or the fact that their pupils are in fact many individual eyes on close inspection). Within a short while, the new corpse-kisser takes its first, shuddering steps and slinks off to finish devouring what is left of the viscera and bones in private.<br /> <br /> Despite their nature as colonies of bugs, breeders actually have individual personalities. They're usually simple, and informed by the basics of eat, sleep, breed, repeat, but many do develop an appreciation for things like art or politics (though their ideas can be a bit...<i>odd</i>). They don't have much in the way of individuality or fear, though-they know that their memories are just a transfer of taxidermites away from safekeeping, and as colonial minds they have a difficult time understanding why one would not sacrifice the self to save the many. They aren't killers, though-taxidermies aren't on a tight schedule if they can keep generating the preservative enzyme (actually <i>easier </i>in the modern era-something about television static catalyzes extremely efficient synthesis of the enzyme), they can wait for death to happen naturally-they aren't particularly attached to any species, and so long as there's at least 70% of skin remaining, they can patch up the disguise (though they may need to hunt in order to make up for missing biomass if their new host is particularly degraded).<br /> <br /> However, corpse-kissers <i>do </i>recognize that human environments are ideal for breeding. Besides being a highly numerous species who are so communal they can find colony-mates to sexually reproduce with (which produces true nymphs-unfertilized eggs hatch straight into the fragile adult stage, and are genetic clones) far more easily than just wandering the woods looking for mates, there's the simple fact that humans preserve their dead, and from there, bury them in convenient spots; most of the chemicals in funeral parlors are not toxic to the taxidermites, who have similar chemicals in their enzymes, so a graveyard or morgue is like a cheap suburb to them.<br /> <br /> However, this presents a problem-getting to the bodies. Ever pragmatic, the hivemen have realized something else-those same humans control access to their bodies, many of whom are supernatural beings themselves, or occultists who, most importantly, need muscle.<br /> <br /> Thus, the parasites have become something of a symbiote-mercenaries work for mutual benefit, don't they?<br /> <br /> (will continue)<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.us/a/img824/1346/mosz.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/180-leliel","name":"Leliel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar180_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2259"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}